## Proposed Solution for Issue #4080

### Root Cause Analysis & Fix
The watcher was creating issues for all existing open bounties on startup because it lacked a persistent record of which bounties had already been processed, causing duplicate issue creation for pre‑existing bounties. By persisting processed bounty IDs to a state file and checking against it before creating an issue, the script now only generates issues for newly opened bounties.

### Proposed Code Patch
```
--- a/watcher.js
+++ b/watcher.js
@@
-const openBounties = await fetchOpenBounties();
-openBounties.forEach(bounty => createIssueForBounty(bounty));
+const path = require('path');
+const fs = require('fs');
+const statePath = path.resolve(__dirname, 'bounty_state.json');
+let processed = new Set();
+if (fs.existsSync(statePath)) {
+  try {
+    processed = new Set(JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(statePath, 'utf8')));
+  } catch (_) {}
+}
+const openBounties = await fetchOpenBounties();
+openBounties.forEach(bounty => {
+  if (processed.has(bounty.id)) return; // Skip already handled bounties
+  createIssueForBounty(bounty);
+  processed.add(bounty.id);
+});
+fs.writeFileSync(statePath, JSON.stringify([...processed]));

```

